      "output": "**Water (H₂O)** is a simple yet profoundly essential chemical compound that makes up the majority of life on Earth and shapes our planet’s climate, geology, and ecosystems. Here’s a concise breakdown of what water is and why it matters:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical Formula** | H₂O – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular Structure** | Bent shape (≈104.5° angle) giving it a polar, “V‑shaped” geometry. |\n| **Polarity & Hydrogen Bonding** | The oxygen atom is slightly negative; the hydrogens are slightly positive. This polarity allows water molecules to form hydrogen bonds, giving water many unique properties. |\n| **States of Matter** | Solid (ice), liquid (water), gas (vapor). Transitions depend on temperature and pressure. |\n| **Key Physical Properties** | • **High specific heat** – absorbs heat without large temperature changes. <br>• **High surface tension** – allows insects to walk on it and creates droplets. <br>• **Excellent solvent** – dissolves many substances, enabling biochemical reactions. <br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating aquatic life in winter. |\n| **Biological Role** | • **Solvent for cells** – carries nutrients, waste, and signals. <br>• **Temperature regulator** – through sweating, respiration, and circulation. <br>• **Chemical medium** – participates in metabolic pathways (e.g., photosynthesis, respiration). |\n| **Geological & Environmental Role** | • **Hydrologic cycle** – evaporation, condensation, precipitation, runoff. <br>• **Erosion & weathering** – shapes landscapes. <br>• **Climate moderator** – stores and transports heat globally. |\n| **Human Use** | • **Drinking & sanitation** – essential for health. <br>• **Agriculture** – irrigation. <br>• **Industry** – cooling, chemical processing, energy generation. <br>• **Cultural & spiritual significance** – rituals, symbolism. |\n| **Scientific Interest** | • **Water’s “anomalies”** (e.g., high boiling point, surface tension) are subjects of ongoing research. <br>• **Water in extreme environments** (deep oceans, ice moons) informs astrobiology. |\n\n### Bottom Line\nWater is a polar, hydrogen‑bonding molecule that exists in three states, has remarkable physical properties, and is indispensable for life, Earth’s climate, and human civilization. Its simple formula belies its complexity and central role in the natural world."
